=== SCHEMA ===
migrations/000172_drives_si.up.sql defines, in order:

  -- defensive cleanup (self-healing if a stale legacy snapshot is reapplied)
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trip_drives CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trips       CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS drives      CASCADE;

  CREATE TABLE drives ( ... );
    23 columns total, all SI-canonical:
      id BIGSERIAL PK, vehicle_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
      started_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL, ended_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
      start_lat / start_lng / end_lat / end_lng DOUBLE PRECISION
        (WGS84 decimal degrees, angular, no SI conversion),
      start_place / end_place TEXT (geocoded labels),
      start_odometer_m / end_odometer_m DOUBLE PRECISION (meters),
      distance_m DOUBLE PRECISION (meters; maintained by close-out
        writer per prompt comment),
      duration_s BIGINT (seconds),
      start_soc_pct / end_soc_pct REAL (0-100 percent),
      energy_used_wh / regen_energy_wh DOUBLE PRECISION (Watt-hours),
      avg_speed_mps / max_speed_mps DOUBLE PRECISION (meters/second),
      avg_power_w / peak_power_w DOUBLE PRECISION (Watts),
      ambient_temp_c_avg DOUBLE PRECISION (Celsius).

    CREATE INDEX drives_vehicle_started ON drives
        (vehicle_id, started_at DESC);

  CREATE TABLE trips ( ... );
    8 columns total:
      id BIGSERIAL PK, vehicle_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
      name TEXT (nullable per prompt),
      started_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL, ended_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
      created_by_user BIGINT (nullable, no FK at this slot),
      auto_generated BOOLEAN DEFAULT false,
      notes TEXT.

    CREATE INDEX trips_vehicle_started ON trips
        (vehicle_id, started_at DESC);

  CREATE TABLE trip_drives ( ... );
    3 columns + composite PK + FKs:
      trip_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES trips(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE
      drive_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES drives(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
      position INT NOT NULL
      PRIMARY KEY (trip_id, drive_id)

    CREATE INDEX trip_drives_drive ON trip_drives (drive_id);

  COMMENT ON TABLE/COLUMN statements document each surface for
  audit-grep-ability.

WHY all three are regular tables (not hypertables):
  Prompt action step 5 explicitly states "None are hypertables (low
  row count)." Drives are bounded by drive frequency (a few per day
  per vehicle), not by telemetry tick rate. Trips are user-curated
  and even rarer. trip_drives is bounded by trips * drives-per-trip.
  None justifies the chunk-management overhead a hypertable carries.

WHY no FK from drives.vehicle_id, charging_sessions.vehicle_id, or
trips.vehicle_id to vehicles(id) at this slot:
  Phase-42 prompts 0030 (positions), 0031 (snapshots), and 0032
  (charging) deliberately omit FK constraints to vehicles(id) so the
  writer hot path can insert rows without taking a referential lock
  against the vehicles table. The 000142 baseline did carry these
  FKs; phase-42 trades that referential safety for ingest throughput.
  A later operational prompt may reinstate the constraints once the
  vehicle ingestion path is settled. This matches the precedent set
  by all four prior phase-42 hot-table migrations.

WHY no FK from drives.id back into drive_telemetry.drive_id:
  Same rationale, applied symmetrically: drive_telemetry carries the
  per-tick rows and must not block on a referential check against
  drives. The drive-summary writer attributes ticks by id without a
  DB-level FK constraint.

WHY trip_drives.position has no UNIQUE constraint per trip:
  The prompt specifies "position INT NOT NULL" with the comment
  "order within trip" but does not mandate a uniqueness constraint.
  A future prompt may add a UNIQUE (trip_id, position) index once
  the trip writer is wired and the gap-handling semantics
  (insert-between, reorder) are decided. For now, the writer is
  responsible for assigning monotonic 1-based positions.

WHY share_tokens is left in place (CASCADE drops only the FK):
  migration 000125_add_share_tokens introduced share_tokens with a
  drive_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES drives(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
  constraint. The CASCADE on `DROP TABLE drives` here drops that FK
  constraint but does not drop the share_tokens table itself
  (PostgreSQL DROP TABLE ... CASCADE drops dependent objects, not
  dependent tables). Existing share_tokens rows therefore lose their
  referential link to the recreated drives table. This is the
  documented forward-only cost — a follow-up prompt either
  reinstates the FK against the new drives schema or migrates
  share_tokens to a phase-42-shaped equivalent.

migrations/000172_drives_si.down.sql:
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trip_drives CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS trips       CASCADE;
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS drives      CASCADE;
  (Forward-only rollback — there is no legacy schema to restore.
  Drop order mirrors the up.sql defensive cleanup.)

